# 9. DONATION REQUEST AND RESERVATION ENGINE

Implement a complete, reliable request workflow.

### Donation Lifecycle

Donor creates food listing
→ Recipient discovers listing
→ Recipient submits request
→ Request becomes pending
→ Donor accepts or rejects
→ Accepted quantity is reserved
→ Pickup is scheduled
→ Recipient collects food
→ Both parties confirm collection
→ Donation becomes completed
→ History and analytics update.

### Request Statuses

- PENDING
- ACCEPTED
- REJECTED
- CANCELLED
- PICKUP_SCHEDULED
- COLLECTED
- COMPLETED

### Business Rules

1. Only verified NGOs can request food.
2. Only available or partially reserved listings can receive requests.
3. Pending requests must not reserve inventory.
4. Accepted requests must reserve inventory.
5. Rejected requests must not change inventory.
6. Accepted requests can be cancelled according to defined cancellation rules before collection.
7. Cancelled accepted requests must restore reserved quantities.
8. Collected food must not be restored to available inventory.
9. Completed requests cannot be modified.
10. Expired listings cannot receive new requests.
11. Donors can only accept or reject requests for their own listings.
12. Recipients can only cancel their own eligible requests.
13. Duplicate requests must be prevented or explicitly handled.

### Concurrency Protection

This is a critical requirement.

Example:

A restaurant has 20 meal boxes.

NGO A requests 15.
NGO B requests 15.

The system must never allow both requests to be accepted if only 20 boxes exist.

Use PostgreSQL database transactions and appropriate row locking or atomic conditional updates.

Validate available quantity at the moment of acceptance.

Update reservation records and listing inventory within the same transaction.

Rollback all related changes if any operation fails.

